w: 1.~Do ye, O con- gre- ga- ti- on, in- deed speak right- eous- ness? O ye that are the sons of men, judge ye with u- prigh- tness?
w: 2.~Yea, ev'n with- in your ver- y hearts ye wick- ed- ness have done; And ye the vi'- lence of your hands do weigh the earth up- on.
w: 3.~The wi- cked men es- trang- èd are, ev'n from the ver- y womb; They, spea- king lies, do stray as soon as to the world they come.
w: 4.~Un- to a ser- pent's poi- son like their poi- son doth ap- pear; Yea, they are like the ad- der deaf, that clo- sely stops her ear;
w: 5.~That so she may not hear the voice of one that charm her would, No, not though he most cun- ning were, and charm most wi- sely could.
w: 6.~Their teeth, O God, with- in their mouth break thou in piec- es small; The great teeth break thou out, O Lord, of these young li- ons all.
w: 7.~Let them like wa- ters melt a- way, which down- ward still do flow: In piec- es cut his ar- rows all, when he shall bend his bow.
w: 8.~Like to a snail that melts a- way, let each of them be gone; Like wo- man's birth un- ti- mely, that they ne- ver see the sun.
w: 9.~He shall them take a- way be- fore your pots the thorns can find, Both liv- ing, and in fu- ry great, as with a storm- y wind.
w: 10.~The right- eous, when he ven- geance sees, he shall be joy- ful then; The right- eous one shall wash his feet in blood of wi- cked men.
w: 11.~So men shall say, The right- eous man re- ward shall ne- ver miss: And ve- ri- ly up- on the earth a God to judge there is.
